# ParadeDB

- [Welcome to ParadeDB](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/start/introduction.md): Just use Postgres
- [Install ParadeDB](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/start/install.md): Run ParadeDB locally
- [Configure Your Environment](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/start/configure-your-environment.md): Connect your preferred SQL client or ORM to ParadeDB
- [Create Your First Index](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/start/create-your-first-index.md): Create a ParadeDB index over your table
- [Run Your First Queries](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/start/run-queries.md): Run your first queries on ParadeDB
- [Load Your Own Data](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/start/load-data.md): Dump data from an existing Postgres and load into ParadeDB
- [Set Up Your Coding Agent](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/start/ai-agents.md): Teach your coding agent to use ParadeDB
- [Go to Production](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/start/go-to-production.md): Move from the sandbox to a production deployment
- [Reference](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/overview.md): Detailed syntax for ParadeDB indexes, queries, aggregates, and SQL APIs
- [Create an Index](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/indexing/create-index.md): Index a Postgres table for ParadeDB search workloads
- [Partial Indexes](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/indexing/indexing-partial.md): Add row filters to the ParadeDB index
- [Columnar Storage](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/indexing/columnar.md): Column-oriented indexing for fast filtering, sorting, and aggregates
- [Index Partitioning](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/indexing/partition-by.md): Partition ParadeDB indexes to accelerate filter and JOIN queries
- [Indexing Expressions](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/indexing/indexing-expressions.md): Add Postgres expressions to the index
- [Indexing JSON](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/indexing/indexing-json.md): Add JSON and JSONB types to the index
- [Indexing Text Arrays](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/indexing/indexing-arrays.md): Add text arrays to the index
- [Indexing 32+ Columns](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/indexing/indexing-composite.md): Use composite types to index more than 32 columns
- [Indexing Vectors](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/indexing/indexing-vectors.md): Vectors live alongside text and filters in the ParadeDB index
- [Faster BM25 Queries](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/indexing/faster-bm25-queries.md): Speed up queries that return the most relevant results
- [Faster Counts](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/indexing/bitmap-postings.md): Speed up count queries for common search terms
- [Full-Text / Lexical Search](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/full-text/overview.md): Full-text query syntax, scoring, filtering, sorting, and highlighting
- [Tokenizers](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/tokenizers/overview.md): Tokenizers split large chunks of text into small, searchable units called tokens
- [Unicode](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/tokenizers/available-tokenizers/unicode.md): The default text tokenizer in ParadeDB
- [Literal](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/tokenizers/available-tokenizers/literal.md): Indexes the text in its raw form, without any splitting or processing
- [Literal Normalized](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/tokenizers/available-tokenizers/literal-normalized.md): Like the literal tokenizer, but allows for token filters
- [Whitespace](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/tokenizers/available-tokenizers/whitespace.md): Tokenizes text by splitting on whitespace
- [Ngram](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/tokenizers/available-tokenizers/ngrams.md): Splits text into small chunks called grams, useful for partial matching
- [Edge Ngram](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/tokenizers/available-tokenizers/edge-ngrams.md): Generates prefix n-grams per word, ideal for search-as-you-type
- [Simple](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/tokenizers/available-tokenizers/simple.md): Splits on any non-alphanumeric character
- [Regex Patterns](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/tokenizers/available-tokenizers/regex.md): Tokenizes text using a regular expression
- [Chinese Compatible](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/tokenizers/available-tokenizers/chinese-compatible.md): A simple tokenizer for Chinese, Japanese, and Korean characters
- [Lindera](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/tokenizers/available-tokenizers/lindera.md): Uses prebuilt dictionaries to tokenize Chinese, Japanese, and Korean text
- [ICU](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/tokenizers/available-tokenizers/icu.md): Splits text according to the Unicode standard
- [Jieba](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/tokenizers/available-tokenizers/jieba.md): The most advanced Chinese tokenizer that leverages both a dictionary and statistical models
- [Source Code](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/tokenizers/available-tokenizers/source-code.md): Tokenizes text that is actually code
- [Token Filters](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/token-filters/overview.md): Configure lowercasing, stemming, ASCII folding, and other token processing
- [Alpha Numeric Only](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/token-filters/alphanumeric.md): Removes any tokens that contain characters that are not ASCII letters or digits
- [ASCII Folding](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/token-filters/ascii-folding.md): Strips away diacritical marks like accents
- [Lowercase](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/token-filters/lowercase.md): Converts all characters to lowercase
- [Remove Stopwords](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/token-filters/stopwords.md): Remove language-specific stopwords from the index
- [Stemmer](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/token-filters/stemming.md): Reduces words to their root form for a given language
- [Token Length](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/token-filters/token-length.md): Remove tokens that are above or below a certain byte length from the index
- [Trim](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/token-filters/trim.md): Remove trailing and leading whitespace from a token
- [Multiple Tokenizers Per Field](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/tokenizers/multiple-per-field.md): Apply different token configurations to the same field
- [Query-Time Tokenizer](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/tokenizers/search-tokenizer.md): Use a different tokenizer at search time than at index time
- [Advanced Query Functions](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/full-text/query-builder.md): Query builder functions for advanced full-text query types
- [Match](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/full-text/match.md): Returns documents that match the provided query string, which is tokenized before matching
- [Term](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/full-text/term.md): Look for exact token matches in the source document, without any further processing of the query string
- [Phrase](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/full-text/phrase.md): Phrase queries are like match queries, but with order and position of matching tokens enforced
- [Proximity](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/full-text/proximity.md): Match documents based on token proximity within the source document
- [Phrase Prefix](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/full-text/phrase-prefix.md): Finds documents containing a phrase followed by a term prefix
- [Regex Phrase](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/full-text/regex-phrase.md): Matches a specific sequence of regex queries
- [Fuzzy](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/full-text/fuzzy.md): Allow for typos in the query string
- [Regex](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/full-text/regex.md): Searches for terms that match a regex pattern
- [Range Term](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/full-text/range-term.md): Filters over Postgres range types
- [More Like This](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/full-text/more-like-this.md): Finds documents that are "like" another document
- [Query Parser](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/full-text/query-parser.md): Accept raw user-provided query strings
- [All](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/full-text/all.md): Search all rows in the index
- [Overview](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/filtering/overview.md): Choose the right filtering path for ParadeDB queries
- [Indexed Columns](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/filtering/indexed.md): Push filters into the ParadeDB index scan
- [External Indexes](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/filtering/external-indexes.md): Combine ParadeDB search with filters backed by other database indexes
- [Top K](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/full-text/top-k.md): ParadeDB is optimized for quickly finding the Top K results in a table
- [BM25 Scoring](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/full-text/score.md): BM25 scores sort the result set by relevance
- [Relevance Tuning](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/full-text/relevance-tuning.md): Tune the BM25 score by adjusting the weights of individual queries
- [Highlighting](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/full-text/highlight.md): Generate snippets for portions of the source text that match the query string
- [Vector / Semantic Search](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/vector/overview.md): Reference pages for indexing, querying, and tuning vector and semantic search
- [Querying Vectors](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/vector/querying.md): Run nearest-neighbor vector search inside the ParadeDB index
- [Tuning Recall and Latency](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/vector/tuning.md): Trade recall against latency for vector search
- [Hybrid Search](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/hybrid/overview.md): Reference pages for combining full-text and vector rankings
- [Reciprocal Rank Fusion](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/hybrid/rrf.md): Combine full text and vector search results into a single ranking
- [Aggregates](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/aggregates/overview.md): Accelerate SQL and JSON aggregates with the ParadeDB index
- [Terms](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/aggregates/bucket/terms.md): Count the number of occurrences for each value in a result set
- [Filters](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/aggregates/bucket/filters.md): Compute aggregations over multiple filters in one query
- [Histogram](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/aggregates/bucket/histogram.md): Count the number of occurrences over some interval
- [Date Histogram](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/aggregates/bucket/datehistogram.md): Count the number of occurrences over fixed time intervals
- [Range](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/aggregates/bucket/range.md): Count the number of occurrences over user-defined buckets
- [Average](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/aggregates/metrics/average.md): Compute the average value of a field
- [Cardinality](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/aggregates/metrics/cardinality.md): Compute the number of distinct values in a field
- [Count](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/aggregates/metrics/count.md): Count the number of values in a field
- [Min/Max](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/aggregates/metrics/minmax.md): Compute the min/max value of a field
- [Percentiles](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/aggregates/metrics/percentiles.md): Analyze the distribution of a field
- [Stats](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/aggregates/metrics/stats.md): Compute several metrics at once
- [Sum](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/aggregates/metrics/sum.md): Compute the sum of a field
- [Top Hits](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/aggregates/metrics/tophits.md): Compute the top hits for each bucket in a terms aggregation
- [Facets](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/aggregates/facets.md): Compute a Top K and aggregate in one query
- [Limitations](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/aggregates/limitations.md): Caveats for aggregate support
- [Supported Join Types](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/joins/overview.md): JOIN types and pushdown requirements supported by ParadeDB
- [Using ParadeDB with Citus](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/sharding/citus.md): Distributed full-text search with Citus and ParadeDB
- [Operator Reference](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/operators-and-functions.md): ParadeDB operators and their SQL function equivalents
- [Configuration Reference](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/configuration.md): Runtime settings for ParadeDB and pg_search
- [SQL Function Reference](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/reference/sql-functions.md): User-facing SQL functions exposed by ParadeDB and pg_search
- [Architecture](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/concepts/architecture.md): A deep dive into how ParadeDB is built
- [Guarantees](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/concepts/guarantees.md): ParadeDB ensures ACID compliance, concurrency, data integrity, and replication safety
- [How Text Search Works](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/concepts/full-text/overview.md): Understand how ParadeDB uses token matching to efficiently search large corpuses of text
- [How Vector Search Works](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/concepts/vector/overview.md): Understand how ParadeDB natively supports vector search
- [How Hybrid Search Works](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/concepts/hybrid/overview.md): Understand how ParadeDB combines text and vector search into a single ranking
- [Limitations & Tradeoffs](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/concepts/limitations.md): Understand ParadeDB's key limitations and tradeoffs
- [ParadeDB Enterprise](https://www.paradedb.com/docs/operate/deploy/enterprise.md): Feature comparison between ParadeDB Community and Enterprise
